<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aquitani</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aquitanians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aquitani were the ancient peoples of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and the Atlantic. Unlike the Celtic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, they spoke a language related to early Basque, known from personal and divine names in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscriptions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s legate Publius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> conquered them in 56 BCE, and their land became part of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Aquitania</a>.</p></div>
